   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.5",
    "topic_title": "Atomic Structure and Electron Configuration",
    "stem": "Sodium's second ionization energy is roughly nine times its first ionization energy. Which choice gives the correct ground-state electron configuration of a neutral sodium atom (Z = 11) and correctly explains this large jump using Coulomb's law?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹; the second ionization energy is large because Na⁺ has a stable noble-gas configuration, and stable configurations resist losing electrons.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The configuration is right, but the explanation stops at a label (stability) and never mentions charge, distance, or shielding. A stable configuration is a description, not a cause, so this reasoning would not earn the Coulomb's law point. Fix: After writing a configuration, state which subshell the electron leaves and compare its distance and shielding to justify the energy."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹; the second electron is also removed from the 3s subshell, but the positive ion pulls on it more strongly.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misreads the configuration: 3s¹ holds only one electron, so once it is removed, no 3s electrons remain. The second electron must come from 2p in the n = 2 shell. A small increase in ion charge on a same-shell electron could not explain a ninefold jump. Fix: Count the electrons left in each subshell after each removal before naming where the next electron comes from."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹; the second electron is removed from the 2p subshell, which is closer to the nucleus and less shielded than 3s, so it is attracted far more strongly.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Filling 1s, 2s, 2p, then 3s places sodium's only n = 3 electron in 3s. After that electron is removed, the next one comes from n = 2. Coulomb's law says force grows as r decreases, and the 2p electrons are shielded only by the 1s electrons. Both effects greatly increase the attraction to the nucleus."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3p¹; the second electron is removed from the 2p subshell, which is closer to the nucleus and less shielded, so it is held more strongly.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is an Aufbau filling-order error. It skips 3s, which is lower in energy than 3p and must fill first, so the configuration is wrong even though the explanation sounds right. Ground-state sodium has its outermost electron in 3s. Fix: Write out the filling order 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p and check that no lower subshell is skipped."
     }
    ]
   },
