   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.5",
    "topic_title": "Atomic Structure and Electron Configuration",
    "stem": "A neutral potassium atom has 19 protons and 19 electrons. In the notation below, [Ar] represents 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶. Which statement correctly gives potassium’s ground-state electron configuration and compares the energy needed to remove its outermost electron with that needed to remove a 1s electron?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "[Ar] 3d¹; the outermost electron requires less energy to remove because its greater average distance from the nucleus and greater shielding weaken its attraction to the nucleus.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Shell-number filling trap: This configuration places an electron in 3d before filling the lower-energy 4s subshell. Aufbau filling follows increasing subshell energy, not numerical shell order alone. Fix: After filling 3p, place the next electron in 4s before adding electrons to 3d."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "[Ar] 4s¹; the outermost electron and a 1s electron require equal energy to remove because both are attracted to the same 19-proton nucleus.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Nuclear-charge-only trap: Sharing the same nucleus does not give electrons equal attraction or equal removal energies. Electron–nucleus distance and shielding differ substantially between 4s and 1s electrons. Fix: Compare both average distance and shielding before using nuclear charge to judge electron attraction."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "[Ar] 4s¹; the outermost electron requires more energy to remove because shielding by inner electrons strengthens its attraction to the nucleus.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-shielding trap: Inner electrons reduce, rather than strengthen, the effective nuclear attraction experienced by the outermost electron. Together with its greater average distance, this shielding makes the 4s electron easier to remove than a 1s electron. Fix: Treat increased shielding as reduced effective nuclear attraction, then connect weaker attraction to lower removal energy."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "[Ar] 4s¹; the outermost electron requires less energy to remove because its greater average distance from the nucleus and greater shielding weaken its attraction to the nucleus.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Aufbau filling places the nineteenth electron in 4s after 3p is filled. Compared with a 1s electron, the 4s electron is farther from the nucleus on average and more shielded by other electrons, so it experiences weaker Coulombic attraction and requires less energy to remove."
     }
    ]
   },
